INT. UNDERWATER HOCKEY POOL - NIGHT

Chlorine haze. A tiled deck. Six lanes taped off with orange cones. Snorkels and fins in rows along the wall.

COACH BEVERLY STANISLAUS, 50s, whistle between her teeth, paces the deck. RUSLAN VOLODIN, 20s, the team captain, sits on the edge with his feet in the water, taping his stick. LOTTIE FAIRBANKS, 19, adjusts her mask, while DEWAYNE PICKETT, 22, the goalie, bounces on his toes and glares at the scoreboard. On the far side, MR. PHAM, 40s, the referee, in a black-and-white striped rash guard, checks his watch and says nothing.

Beverly stops pacing. She blows the whistle once. Ruslan looks up. Lottie flinches. Dewayne turns from the board and starts toward her, but she holds up a hand and he stops.

BEVERLY
Four seconds on the clock. Down one. Who wants it?

RUSLAN
I do.

DEWAYNE
You always do.

RUSLAN
Somebody has to.

LOTTIE
I'll take it, Coach.

BEVERLY
You'll take the pass.

LOTTIE
That's what I said.

DEWAYNE
(under his breath)
That's not what you said.

Lottie's mask snaps down. Ruslan rises, water streaming off his legs.

RUSLAN
Say it again, Dewayne.

DEWAYNE
I said it was a bad idea. Four seconds, one shot, all on you.

RUSLAN
Then block better.

DEWAYNE
I'm a goalie, not a miracle.

Beverly steps between them. Pham finally blows his own whistle, a thin shriek. Everyone turns.

PHAM
Gentlemen. Ladies. Positions.

They slip into the water in single file, silent. Beverly stands on the deck, hands on her hips, watching the surface close over their heads.

Under the lights, the pool goes still. Then a ripple. Then a roar of bubbles.
